{
  "id": "bounded-buffer-trace",
  "title": "Bounded buffer schedule trace",
  "pattern": "Design & concurrency",
  "level": "sse",
  "difficulty": "stretch",
  "lessonId": "queues-fifo-simulation",
  "paths": [
    "senior"
  ],
  "prompt": "Simulate producers and consumers sharing a bounded FIFO buffer in discrete ticks, and return which consumer took which item at which tick.\n\n- producers[i] = [gap, items]: producer i puts its items into the buffer in order. consumers[j] = gap for consumer j. Every gap is 1 to 10; capacity is 1 to 100.\n- Ticks are numbered 1, 2, 3, ... Every producer and consumer may first try at tick 1. Each tick has two phases, in this order:\n  1. Produce: for i = 0, 1, ..., producer i acts if it has items left, may try (tick >= its next try tick) and the buffer holds fewer than capacity items. Acting appends its next item to the back of the buffer and sets its next try tick to tick + gap. A producer that may try but finds the buffer full simply tries again next tick.\n  2. Consume: for j = 0, 1, ..., consumer j acts if it may try and the buffer is non-empty. Acting removes the front item, records [tick, j, item] and sets its next try tick to tick + gap. An item produced earlier in the same tick can be consumed in that tick.\n- The simulation ends when every item has been consumed. Return the records in the order they were made. There are 0 to 50 producers, 1 to 50 consumers and up to 5,000 items in total; gaps can leave long idle stretches, so do not assume every tick matters.",
  "function": {
    "js": "bufferTrace",
    "py": "buffer_trace"
  },
  "starter": {
    "js": "function bufferTrace(capacity, producers, consumers) {\n  // Your code here\n}\n",
    "py": "def buffer_trace(capacity, producers, consumers):\n    # Your code here\n    pass\n"
  },
